% ==============================================================================
% \zlim + * {var} * {limit}
% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Limit operator: "lim" with the script `var \to limit`. Without the plus the
% script is centred BELOW the operator in every style, as \lim\limits_{...}
% would in display; with a leading + it hangs to the LOWER RIGHT of the
% operator, as a subscript (\lim\nolimits_{...}). One-sided limits are
% written in {limit} itself, e.g. {0^+}. Safe in text, inline, and display
% mode.
%
% The script is a box set one style below that of the operator and placed
% explicitly, never by TeX's limits machinery, so every style shares one
% geometry and the script is free to overlap the operator. A star before
% {var} or {limit} additionally scales that part, as a box inside the
% script, by `zlim-scale-<style>`, where <style> (display, text, script,
% scriptscript) is the style of the OPERATOR; the same four factors apply to
% both parts. The switch `zlim-scale-absolute` (see GLOBAL) selects the
% meaning: true (default) -> normal size x factor; false -> unstarred script
% size x factor. The defaults are absolute factors derived as in "Choosing
% the factors" in GLOBAL. A starred part is guarded against shrinking below
% the scriptscript size when nested in other scaled boxes (see
% "Nesting guard" in GLOBAL); unstarred parts are floored by TeX itself.
%
% Every position is measured from the EDGES of the operator to the NEAREST
% edge of the script, as a dimensionless RATIO of a unit measured in the
% style of the operator (see GLOBAL): vertical options (v, above, below) are
% ratios of its x-height, horizontal ones (h, before, after) of its quad.
%
% Option names: zlim-<mode>-<form>-<axis>, with
%   mode   math (display style) | text (text, script and scriptscript style)
%   form   below (unstarred by +) | script (with +)
%   axis   v (vertical) | h (horizontal)
% and zlim-<mode>-<before|after|above|below> for the frame. Every key
% exists for both modes. The scales zlim-scale-<style> and the switch
% zlim-scale-absolute are mode-free.
%
% Coordinates: x positive to the right, y positive upward.
%   v  = y of the script's TOP edge, from the operator's BOTTOM edge
%        (negative lowers the script below the operator).
%   h  = below: shift of the script from its centred position;
%        script: x of the script's LEFT edge, from the operator's RIGHT edge.
%
% The whole output is one \mathop box (followed by \nolimits, so TeX keeps
% the usual space after an operator) whose height and depth cover the
% operator and the script, plus `above`/`below`, and whose width covers the
% same plus `before`/`after` (all signed and additive, as in \zscript), so
% neighbouring lines are pushed apart. A negative `after` removes part of
% the space left after the operator, pulling the following term (e.g. a
% derivative) closer. The box is wrapped in \mathop OUTSIDE the \mathchoice,
% so a script or \left/\right attached afterwards sees its true height.
%
%   \zlim{x}{0}              \zlim+{x}{0}
%   \zlim*{x}*{0^+}          \zlim+*{n}{\infty}
% ==============================================================================

  % \msz@lim@opt {key suffix} {macro suffix} {default} — declares the
  % option macro \msz@lim@<macro suffix> and its key zlim-<key suffix>
  \newcommand{\msz@lim@opt}[3]{%
    \expandafter\newcommand\csname msz@lim@#2\endcsname{#3}%
    \define@key{shorthand}{zlim-#1}{\expandafter\renewcommand\csname msz@lim@#2\endcsname{##1}}%
  }

