% ==============================================================================
% \zint *[lower] *[upper] * * [type]
% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Configurable integral operator (no body argument — place it immediately
% before the integrand, exactly as \int itself). TYPE selects the glyph
% cluster: "1" (default) a single integral, "2".."n" n tightly spaced
% integrals (surface, volume, ...), "o" a contour \oint, anything else a
% bridged \int \cdots \int. The (up to two) stars immediately before [type]
% select the placement CLASS, independently of math/text style: none -> slant
% (default), one -> stack, two -> right. A star before [lower]/[upper]
% additionally scales that limit by `zint-scale-<style>`, where <style>
% (display, text, script, scriptscript) is the style of the GLYPH; the same
% four factors apply to both limits. The switch `zint-scale-absolute` (see
% GLOBAL) selects the meaning: true (default) -> normal size x factor; false
% -> unstarred limit size x factor. The defaults are absolute factors derived
% as in "Choosing the factors" in GLOBAL. A starred limit is guarded against
% shrinking below the scriptscript size when nested in other scaled
% boxes (see "Nesting guard" in GLOBAL); unstarred limits are floored by TeX
% itself.
%
% Both limits are always boxes placed explicitly, never by TeX's script
% machinery, so every combination of mode and class shares one geometry, and
% limits are free to overlap the glyph.
%
% Every position and frame margin is a dimensionless RATIO of a unit measured
% in the style of the glyph cluster (see GLOBAL): vertical options (v, above,
% below) are ratios of its x-height, horizontal ones (h, before, after) of its
% quad, so one setting looks the same at any style, including inside
% exponents. The mu kerns `-separation`, `-dots-left` and `-dots-right`
% already follow the style and remain mu lengths.
%
% Option names: zint-<mode>-<class>-<limit>-<axis>, with
%   mode   math (display style) | text (text, script and scriptscript style)
%   class  slant | stack | right
%   limit  top (upper limit) | bottom (lower limit)
%   axis   v (vertical) | h (horizontal)
% and zint-<mode>-<separation|dots-left|dots-right> for the cluster and
% zint-<mode>-<before|after|above|below> for the frame. Every key exists for
% every mode/class combination. The scales zint-scale-<style> and the switch
% zint-scale-absolute are mode-free.
%
% Coordinates: one Cartesian system for every limit — x positive to the
% right, y positive upward. Vertically the origin is always the vertical
% centre of the glyph cluster; `...-v` is the y of the limit's NEAREST edge
% (lower edge of a top limit, upper edge of a bottom limit), so top values
% are positive and bottom values negative. Horizontally the origin and the
% limit's anchored edge depend on the class:
%   stack  origin = horizontal centre of the whole cluster; the limit's own
%          CENTRE is anchored, so one value suits limits of any width.
%   right  origin = centre of the rightmost glyph (the glyph centre for a
%          single integral, so it does not depend on the number of
%          integrals); the LEFT edge of both limits is anchored, so wider
%          limits grow away from the glyph.
%   slant  origin DIFFERS per limit, to keep a cluster's limits fixed
%          relative to its outer silhouette regardless of how many glyphs it
%          has: for the bottom limit, origin = the cluster's LEFT edge and
%          the limit's RIGHT edge is anchored (negative h moves it further
%          left); for the top limit, origin = the cluster's RIGHT edge and
%          the limit's LEFT edge is anchored (positive h moves it further
%          right). This keeps a 1- and a 2-integral cluster's limits from
%          jumping when only the type changes.
%
% The whole output is one box whose height and depth cover the cluster and
% both limits, plus `above`/`below`, and whose width covers the same plus
% `before`/`after` (all signed and additive, as in \zscript), so neighbouring
% lines are pushed apart while inner pieces may still overlap each other. A
% negative `after` removes part of the italic correction kept in the glyph's
% width. `-separation` is the kern between consecutive \intop glyphs;
% `-dots-left` and `-dots-right` are the kerns before and after the interior
% \cdots of a bridged cluster, kept apart because the slant of the glyphs
% makes the optical gaps on its two sides differ. The box is wrapped in \mathop
% OUTSIDE the \mathchoice, so a script or \left/\right attached afterwards
% sees its true height.
%
%   \zint[0][T] f(t)\,dt        \zint*[a][b] f(x)\,dx
%   \zint[a][b][2] f\,dA        \zint[a][b]**[o] \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{r}
% ==============================================================================

  % \msz@int@opt {key suffix} {macro suffix} {default} — declares the option
  % macro \msz@int@<macro suffix> and its key zint-<key suffix> in one go
  \newcommand{\msz@int@opt}[3]{%
    \expandafter\newcommand\csname msz@int@#2\endcsname{#3}%
    \define@key{shorthand}{zint-#1}{\expandafter\renewcommand\csname msz@int@#2\endcsname{##1}}%
  }

  \msz@int@opt{math-separation}{math@separation}{-20mu}
  \msz@int@opt{math-dots-left}{math@dots@left}{-20mu}
  \msz@int@opt{math-dots-right}{math@dots@right}{-10mu}
  \msz@int@opt{math-before}{math@before}{0}
  \msz@int@opt{math-after}{math@after}{-0.2}
